Starter PIDoc source for Astro Color Mixer v0.9.3-beta. Formal PixInsight documentation integration to be validated.

\title Astro Color Mixer Technical Appendix

\section Introduction

This appendix summarizes the practical processing model used by Astro Color Mixer for nonlinear RGB astrophotography refinement.

\section Design Goals

- controlled nonlinear RGB refinement
- astro-specific color-band workflow
- reduced arbitrary global color swings
- support for stars-present and starless workflows
- visible masks and diagnostics
- non-destructive output-to-new-image behavior

\section Processing Assumptions

- nonlinear RGB input
- normalized internal RGB values in the range 0..1
- source image not overwritten
- preview uses downsampled data
- final Apply uses full-resolution data

\section Image Type

Stars Present is more conservative around bright stellar structures. Starless allows more freedom in nebulae, galaxies, dust, and faint structures after stars have been removed. This setting does not detect stars, remove stars, or create a star mask.

\section Luminance Model

Y = 0.2126 R + 0.7152 G + 0.0722 B

Luminance is used for Range Mask, diagnostics, neutral luminance, and protection terms.

\section Hue and Saturation Selection

Hue is circular and low saturation makes hue unreliable. Band selection uses circular hue distance around practical astro band centers.

\section Astro Color Bands

- red 0 deg
- orange 30 deg
- yellow 60 deg
- green 120 deg
- cyan 180 deg
- blue 240 deg
- purple 275 deg
- magenta 315 deg

These are practical editing regions, not strict physical line definitions.

\section Hue Band Mask

distance = circularHueDistance(hue, center)
mask = 1 - smoothstep(innerWidth, outerWidth, distance)

\section Selected Band Spatial Blur

Selected Band Blur is an optional spatial blur applied to the active band's final mask. It is not part of hue selection itself and is not a luminance Range Mask control.

Feather softens selection as hue distance approaches the edge of the selected band. Range Mask Feather softens luminance inclusion at the low and high range boundaries. Selected Band Blur smooths the already-built band mask across neighboring image pixels.

The implementation uses modest whole-pixel radii only: Off, 1 px, 2 px, 3 px, 4 px, or 5 px. It is applied only when Image Type is Starless. In Stars Present mode, saved blur values are ignored because spatially blurring a color mask can leak adjustments into star cores, halos, and adjacent stellar structures.

rawBandMask = hueMask * saturationReliability * protection * rangeMask
if imageType == starless and selectedBandBlur > 0:
    workingBandMask = spatialBlur(rawBandMask, selectedBandBlur)
else:
    workingBandMask = rawBandMask

\section Saturation Reliability

Low-saturation pixels are prevented from behaving like stable hue targets, which is why the tool includes a separate Neutral / Low-Saturation luminance path.

\section Range Mask

leftRamp = smoothstep(low - feather, low, Y)
rightRamp = 1 - smoothstep(high, high + feather, Y)
rangeMask = clamp01(leftRamp * rightRamp)

\section Neutral / Low-Saturation

neutralMask = 1 - smoothstep(satStart, satFull, saturation)

\section Low-Saturation Galaxy Case

Some broadband galaxies have real but weak color in outer arms, dust, and faint halos. Protect Low Sat reduces the response of hue-band sliders when saturation is low because hue is less reliable there.

Turning off Protect Low Sat can help extract faint galaxy color, but it also increases the risk of blotchy color, noisy hue patches, and colored star halos. For aggressive galaxy or dust work, use a starless image when possible, inspect the Band Mask and Combined Mask, and prefer multiple smaller passes over one extreme pass.

\section Chroma-Vector Adjustment

Conceptually, the tool separates a luminance-like neutral component from chroma, scales saturation, rotates hue, adjusts luminance, recombines the result, and clamps back into a valid nonlinear RGB range.

\section Combined Mask

finalMask =
  hueMask *
  saturationReliability *
  darkProtection *
  highlightProtection *
  rangeMask

If Selected Band Blur is active, the band mask is spatially blurred after these selection terms are combined and before the hue, saturation, or luminance adjustment is applied.

\section Refinement Passes

working = original
for each enabled pass:
    working = applyPass(working, pass)

\section Limitations

- not intended for linear calibration
- preview is approximate
- hue is unreliable in neutral areas
- Range Mask depends on stretch
- saturated star cores require care
- beta software status applies
